            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Welcome!\r\n\r\n> As i understand it right now \"E4\" means there are 4 errors, ...\r\n\r\nCorrect, here is [the description](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/855fdb116408085b7a00be8511c566cdc33648b7/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L263-L265).\r\n\r\n> ... but on top of that I would like to know if I can see the actual lines.\r\n\r\nIt is possible *in theory*, but I wouldn't recommend having that. Statusline content is meant to be quickly executed and making that information available quickly is not entirely straightforward (requires caching data inside a dedicated `DiagnosticChanged` event and then going through the process of customizing [whole statusline content](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/855fdb116408085b7a00be8511c566cdc33648b7/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L82-L1070)).\r\n\r\nWhat I *would* recommend is to use a dedicated mapping that navigates to the first error (or any diagnostic in general) in the current buffer. You can do that with 'mini.bracketed' and its [`diagnostic` target](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/855fdb116408085b7a00be8511c566cdc33648b7/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L269):\r\n- Pressing `[D` will navigate to the first diagnostic in the current *regardless of its type* (error, warning, info, or hint).\r\n- Create custom mapping to navigate errors. Something like this:\r\n    ```lua\r\n    local make_bracketed_error = function(lhs, direction, desc)\r\n      local rhs = function()\r\n        MiniBracketed.diagnostic(direction, { severity = { vim.diagnostic.severity.ERROR } })\r\n      end\r\n      vim.keymap.set('n', lhs, rhs, { desc = desc })\r\n    end\r\n\r\n    make_bracketed_error('[E', 'first', 'First error')\r\n    make_bracketed_error('[e', 'backward', 'Previous error')\r\n    make_bracketed_error(']e', 'forward', 'Next error')\r\n    make_bracketed_error(']E', 'last', 'Last error')\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n    This way you can press `[E` to go to first error in the buffer, `2[E` to the second, `[e` - to the previous, `]e` - to the next, `]E` to the last.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Welcome!</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">As i understand it right now \"E4\" means there are 4 errors, ...</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Correct, here is <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/855fdb116408085b7a00be8511c566cdc33648b7/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L263-L265\">the description</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">... but on top of that I would like to know if I can see the actual lines.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It is possible <em>in theory</em>, but I wouldn't recommend having that. Statusline content is meant to be quickly executed and making that information available quickly is not entirely straightforward (requires caching data inside a dedicated <code class=\"notranslate\">DiagnosticChanged</code> event and then going through the process of customizing <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/855fdb116408085b7a00be8511c566cdc33648b7/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L82-L1070\">whole statusline content</a>).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">What I <em>would</em> recommend is to use a dedicated mapping that navigates to the first error (or any diagnostic in general) in the current buffer. You can do that with 'mini.bracketed' and its <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/855fdb116408085b7a00be8511c566cdc33648b7/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L269\"><code class=\"notranslate\">diagnostic</code> target</a>:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">[D</code> will navigate to the first diagnostic in the current <em>regardless of its type</em> (error, warning, info, or hint).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Create custom mapping to navigate errors. Something like this:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local make_bracketed_error = function(lhs, direction, desc)\n  local rhs = function()\n    MiniBracketed.diagnostic(direction, { severity = { vim.diagnostic.severity.ERROR } })\n  end\n  vim.keymap.set('n', lhs, rhs, { desc = desc })\nend\n\nmake_bracketed_error('[E', 'first', 'First error')\nmake_bracketed_error('[e', 'backward', 'Previous error')\nmake_bracketed_error(']e', 'forward', 'Next error')\nmake_bracketed_error(']E', 'last', 'Last error')\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">make_bracketed_error</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">lhs</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">direction</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">desc</span>)\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">rhs</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>()\n    <span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniBracketed</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">diagnostic</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">direction</span>, { <span class=\"pl-smi\">severity</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> { <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">diagnostic</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">severity</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">ERROR</span> } })\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">vim</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">keymap</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">set</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>n<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">lhs</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">rhs</span>, { <span class=\"pl-smi\">desc</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">desc</span> })\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">make_bracketed_error</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>[E<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>first<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>First error<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>)\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">make_bracketed_error</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>[e<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>backward<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Previous error<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>)\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">make_bracketed_error</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>]e<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>forward<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Next error<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>)\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">make_bracketed_error</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>]E<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>last<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Last error<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>)</pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This way you can press <code class=\"notranslate\">[E</code> to go to first error in the buffer, <code class=\"notranslate\">2[E</code> to the second, <code class=\"notranslate\">[e</code> - to the previous, <code class=\"notranslate\">]e</code> - to the next, <code class=\"notranslate\">]E</code> to the last.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
            "bodyText": "Welcome!\n\nAs i understand it right now \"E4\" means there are 4 errors, ...\n\nCorrect, here is the description.\n\n... but on top of that I would like to know if I can see the actual lines.\n\nIt is possible in theory, but I wouldn't recommend having that. Statusline content is meant to be quickly executed and making that information available quickly is not entirely straightforward (requires caching data inside a dedicated DiagnosticChanged event and then going through the process of customizing whole statusline content).\nWhat I would recommend is to use a dedicated mapping that navigates to the first error (or any diagnostic in general) in the current buffer. You can do that with 'mini.bracketed' and its diagnostic target:\n\n\nPressing [D will navigate to the first diagnostic in the current regardless of its type (error, warning, info, or hint).\n\n\nCreate custom mapping to navigate errors. Something like this:\nlocal make_bracketed_error = function(lhs, direction, desc)\n  local rhs = function()\n    MiniBracketed.diagnostic(direction, { severity = { vim.diagnostic.severity.ERROR } })\n  end\n  vim.keymap.set('n', lhs, rhs, { desc = desc })\nend\n\nmake_bracketed_error('[E', 'first', 'First error')\nmake_bracketed_error('[e', 'backward', 'Previous error')\nmake_bracketed_error(']e', 'forward', 'Next error')\nmake_bracketed_error(']E', 'last', 'Last error')\nThis way you can press [E to go to first error in the buffer, 2[E to the second, [e - to the previous, ]e - to the next, ]E to the last.",
